 The other end. `Works chapter WorkServer` answers "give me the work with hash X"
 and nothing has ever asked it, because nothing listens. This listens.

 It is the smaller half of peer fetch and deliberately so. The asking half is
 the host's -- the compiler cannot fetch a peer itself (Library Rule 2 fixes
 `codex.foreword -> codex -> codex.os -> apps`, so citing the net stack from the
 compiler inverts the order), and the decision taken 2026-07-16 is that it never
 will: the host fetches and hands the work over in the `%%QUOTED-WORKS%%` blob
 the compiler already reads. That leaves this binary as the whole of the server
 side, and it needs to be nothing more than a socket around `serve-work`.

 The index is UNVERIFIED, and that is the honest choice rather than an omission.
 `repo-index-verified` filters a store down to the works whose author a manifest
 admits; this serves whatever is on its disk. A server has no business deciding
 what an asker may believe. The asker hashes what comes back and refuses it
 unless it addresses the hash asked for (`accept-reply`), and its import gate
 checks the signature, the pinned key and the trust floor. Serving an unadmitted
 work cannot hurt the asker, and refusing one here would only mean a peer's trust
 policy silently truncating somebody else's. Content addressing and trust are two
 checks; this binary owns neither.

 The index is built ONCE, before the loop. Rebuilding it per request would
 re-read the whole fact log for every quotation.

 We say:

Section: Identity

 The reply carries a `from`, and nobody checks it -- `accept-reply` ignores it,
 because the reply is answerable by its own content. It is filled in honestly
 anyway: a fixed tool key, the same shape `cdx-store` signs with. A deployment
 serves under the box's own identity.

  press : Keypair
  press = generate-keypair (sha256 (text-to-bytes "codex-serve-tool"))

  serve-id : AgentIdentity
  serve-id = AgentIdentity { public-key = press.public-key }

Section: The Socket

 One connection at a time, the pattern codex-vm port forwarding expects: boot
 with `-portfwd <host>:9300` and the VM injects a SYN toward the guest listener
 for every host client that connects. The gateway MAC is seeded into the ARP
 cache so the SYN-ACK never waits on resolution.

  serve-port : Integer = 9300

 These four addresses are codex-vm's NAT constants. Three of them
 (`local-mac` / `local-ip` / `gateway-ip`) already exist in `Net chapter
 WebServer`, which is where `apps/ideas` reads them from, and citing that chapter
 is how this one first tried to get them. At the time it could not: WebServer's
 loops let-bound effectful `net-io-*` calls in signatures declaring `[Console]`
 and nothing else, which is CDX2031/CDX2033 undeclared-effect-row breakage
 sitting in `codex/os/net` rather than in an app. Those rows are declared now --
 `web-loop-http` and `web-loop-framed` both carry
 `[Console, Network.Read, Network.Write]` -- so WebServer compiles and the
 reason for the copy is gone.

 They are still spelled again here, and that is a real cost -- a fourth copy of an
 address that should have one home, and the home is not an HTTP chapter anyway.
 These belong in `NetworkStack`, where the NIC's own addresses belong; move them
 there and cite them.

  local-mac : List Integer = [82, 84, 0, 18, 52, 86]
  local-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 15]
  gateway-ip : List Integer = [10, 0, 2, 2]

  nat-gw-mac : List Integer = [82, 85, 10, 0, 2, 2]

  fresh-transport : Integer -> TcpTransportState
  fresh-transport (x) =
    let sess = net-session-new local-mac local-ip gateway-ip serve-port 0 gateway-ip 1000
    in let sess2 = set-arp sess (arp-cache-add (sess.arp) gateway-ip nat-gw-mac)
    in transport-new sess2

Section: Answering

 `net-io-accept`, `net-io-recv-loop`, `net-io-send` and `net-io-close` all carry
 Network rows, so every one of them is bound with `<-` inside an act and every
 function that reaches them declares the rows it inherits. The neighbouring
 server in `apps/ideas` let-binds these in pure signatures; that is the
 undeclared-effect-row breakage described above, not a pattern to copy.

  reply-with : WorkIndex, TcpTransportState, WorkRequestMsg -> [Network.Write] Text
  reply-with (ix) (ts) (req) =
    let reply = serve-work ix serve-id req
    in act
      ts1 <- net-io-send ts tag-work-reply (encode-work-reply-body reply)
      ts2 <- net-io-close ts1
      let verdict = if reply-is-empty reply then "miss " else "served "
      in verdict & req.hash
    end

  answer : WorkIndex, TcpTransportState, AgentMessage -> [Network.Write] Text
  answer (ix) (ts) (m) =
    when m
      is MsgWorkRequest (req) -> reply-with ix ts req
      is otherwise -> "not-a-request"

 A tag that is not a work request is dropped rather than answered. `serve-work`
 is the only verb this binary offers, so a peer that framed something else at it
 gets nothing back and the connection closes.

  handle-conn : WorkIndex, TcpTransportState -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Text
  handle-conn (ix) (ts) = act
    r <- net-io-recv-loop ts 0
    if r.has-message == False then "no-message"
    else if r.tag /= tag-work-request then "not-a-request"
    else answer ix (r.transport) (decode-agent-msg (r.tag) (r.body))
  end

  serve-one : WorkIndex -> [Network.Read, Network.Write] Text
  serve-one (ix) = act
    ts <- net-io-accept (net-io-listen (fresh-transport 0)) 0
    when (ts.session).conn.state
      is TcpEstablished -> handle-conn ix ts
      is otherwise -> "no-conn"
  end

Section: Entry

  serve-loop : WorkIndex, Integer -> [Network.Read, Network.Write, Console.Write] Integer
  serve-loop (ix) (count) = act
    diag <- serve-one ix
    print-line-uni ("serve " & show count & " " & diag)
    serve-loop ix (count + 1)
  end

  opening : [Device.Block, Network.Read, Network.Write, Console.Write] Nothing = act
    let ix = repo-index-from-disk disk-load
    in act
      print-line-uni ("cdx-serve: " & show (list-length (ix.wi-paths)) & " paths indexed, listening on guest port " & show serve-port)
      served <- serve-loop ix 0
      print-line-uni ("cdx-serve closed after " & show served)
    end
  end
